<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">French Establishments in Oceania</h1><p class="st-meta">1842 CE – 1946 CE · capital Papeete</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's scattered Pacific colony of Tahiti, the Marquesas and other island groups, which became French Polynesia after the Second World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1842 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas</a> and imposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti</a>, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-tahitian-war">Franco-Tahitian War</a> of 1844 to 1847. In 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-v">Pōmare V</a> ceded Tahiti outright, and France then annexed the Gambier Islands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands-society-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, where the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-raiatea">Kingdom of Raiatea</a> resisted until 1897, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austral-islands">Austral Islands</a> by 1901.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a> ran the colony, with Catholic and Protestant missions dominant in schooling. Copra, vanilla, pearl shell and phosphate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makatea">Makatea</a> were the main exports, and Chinese labourers came for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantation in the 1860s and stayed as traders. The painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin">Paul Gauguin</a> made its image famous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1940 the colony rallied to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free France</a> and sent volunteers to the Pacific Battalion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in which the colony served as an Allied base on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, the Establishments became an overseas territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Union</a> in 1946, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> gained French citizenship. The territory was renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-polynesia">French Polynesia</a> in 1957.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-establishments-in-oceania.jpg" alt="French Establishments in Oceania" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Polynesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1842 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Papeete</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Armand Joseph Bruat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Tahitian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Établissements français de l'Océanie, French Oceania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=french-establishments-in-oceania" aria-label="French Establishments in Oceania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="618 2829 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Establishments in Oceania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M938 3085L938 3085M938 3085L868 3047M938 3085L890 3055M938 3085L878 3055M938 3085L5054 728M938 3085L938 3085"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M938 3085h0M868 3047h0M890 3055h0M878 3055h0M5054 728h0M938 3085h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M938 3085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M938 3085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Papeete. <a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=french-establishments-in-oceania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
